9fbf" "q-138090/selection/c07fcdda3b4195d3397e6a2b060e9dbeaca3895db6cf3c051450e54ec81120a2" "q-138090/selection/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-138090" :question/id #uuid "aacc9ef4-d33c-5e64-bc37-708eaf7597fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-138090" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-138090/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-138090"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection/e995575b247f62c0b0365b012be8221675e92d23a0b23b677280bc4217695ae0" :answer/id #uuid "86b850d9-352b-5e0f-b1e1-b7e31d77f14d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection/abd3d52c3b6309f7a476aeb9c94585a2dbb48b12738272075412a8cf5aae8dae" :answer/id #uuid "5e30cf36-1b23-50b6-aef1-295e10dd303e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection/6802299e98653bb2bdb5ea1be4a1f1e0d5bc96780051693ab1b169d91cc7b522" :answer/id #uuid "ce85591a-508f-5842-ab3e-550d53218916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" :answer/id #uuid "ac5db958-6cb3-5e15-a56e-a8b11b7fd0f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection/799242775b66403259753b66ce92e69b3d37bdb26a0873e1805dc7af386595bb" :answer/id #uuid "b18a0115-ba78-5901-89f1-c89469e41080" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "556b57d3-58c9-514e-89fc-26016ad93407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170412/selection/6802299e98653bb2bdb5ea1be4a1f1e0d5bc96780051693ab1b169d91cc7b522" :answer-field/choices ["q-170412/selection/e995575b247f62c0b0365b012be8221675e92d23a0b23b677280bc4217695ae0" "q-170412/selection/abd3d52c3b6309f7a476aeb9c94585a2dbb48b12738272075412a8cf5aae8dae" "q-170412/selection/6802299e98653bb2bdb5ea1be4a1f1e0d5bc96780051693ab1b169d91cc7b522" "q-170412/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" "q-170412/selection/799242775b66403259753b66ce92e69b3d37bdb26a0873e1805dc7af386595bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170412" :question/id #uuid "8662f12a-f0d6-5702-b0bc-82042dd99b6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-170412" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+14{x}_{3}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-170412/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170412"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection/ab0481631d7ffc87457ac1acbb53deb0442e9e48709dea44725207bead75297e" :answer/id #uuid "68d9efa0-4601-5cf2-ac5a-67fab900517b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection/8a5742d9eb71dce905b2059802d9a4433ae0414ad52a3f551109dbacfdb1e166" :answer/id #uuid "c5e50409-4aa0-5737-90b3-95545eb26ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection/bd7564d2eae2da100bedddf744266761fd8dcc3ac930085e22ae0f5fec987573" :answer/id #uuid "89fc04c3-3025-58b9-8b9e-244b58fa1c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "fc3e52ec-265e-5558-91e1-a3a3b18fc8e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" :answer/id #uuid "c7118295-df5b-54ee-a636-2ccb229da0b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c522d02f-8dad-590a-aabd-6448a46a88fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170411/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" :answer-field/choices ["q-170411/selection/ab0481631d7ffc87457ac1acbb53deb0442e9e48709dea44725207bead75297e" "q-170411/selection/8a5742d9eb71dce905b2059802d9a4433ae0414ad52a3f551109dbacfdb1e166" "q-170411/selection/bd7564d2eae2da100bedddf744266761fd8dcc3ac930085e22ae0f5fec987573" "q-170411/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-170411/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170411" :question/id #uuid "8daa1f82-58aa-5738-bbe7-9b509869abcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-170411" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170411/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection/ab0481631d7ffc87457ac1acbb53deb0442e9e48709dea44725207bead75297e" :answer/id #uuid "eaa21026-cd7b-5b99-9d72-d4ae49fed766" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" :answer/id #uuid "079efc67-78ee-55d3-b210-88b18147526d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection/c07fcdda3b4195d3397e6a2b060e9dbeaca3895db6cf3c051450e54ec81120a2" :answer/id #uuid "5ea54b07-b12c-5b6c-b733-b2985605db92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection/bd7564d2eae2da100bedddf744266761fd8dcc3ac930085e22ae0f5fec987573" :answer/id #uuid "5b1924a0-d95d-5c57-b333-d7c2c8261c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection/c251f28f39e30ac61ce22812928000a365cc115d7ebed6251c06dbb03ad57dfb" :answer/id #uuid "9720cc8a-6f50-5e40-b570-68b80bf90ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "001bf16e-440c-5684-b638-0e6ee4df773d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170410/selection/ab0481631d7ffc87457ac1acbb53deb0442e9e48709dea44725207bead75297e" :answer-field/choices ["q-170410/selection/ab0481631d7ffc87457ac1acbb53deb0442e9e48709dea44725207bead75297e" "q-170410/selection/232791baaa35a9a868d014677e2796393ac4210c37c7f9b35dff934068ec502b" "q-170410/selection/c07fcdda3b4195d3397e6a2b060e9dbeaca3895db6cf3c051450e54ec81120a2" "q-170410/selection/bd7564d2eae2da100bedddf744266761fd8dcc3ac930085e22ae0f5fec987573" "q-170410/selection/c251f28f39e30ac61ce22812928000a365cc115d7ebed6251c06dbb03ad57dfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170410" :question/id #uuid "53e594f2-a0b2-5646-8d6f-4051fd08942a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170410" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-170410/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170410"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection/05cdb65323b80c9fe96dd63e3181250fca61c519305420bd842eaeed4cc88a0f" :answer/id #uuid "b1e11163-10ae-5c9f-960e-093d75c66385" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection/2916fcc150b54de80aa3d5d91f8869562405e4759e5b7f03de64a70da448efac" :answer/id #uuid "0e2c24dd-e47c-5f06-a838-69802d22777f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "7a864133-315c-5519-956f-be7fa1bcf07c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection/0dea6aa52ab2677bd869547653198fc6941279365f5067f18168268586cb5eaf" :answer/id #uuid "f928e613-2ccb-5aba-8e18-13a3a9d29e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection/e01ddc4ceb240ab06a2e0404b37f4954b86c7d168700faeaa18a1d3fb040e65f" :answer/id #uuid "6444f832-19c7-52c3-80a8-2e34a8b1a2f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc3ea7b-9e3e-5177-a4a2-1d666c360cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-138132/selection/e01ddc4ceb240ab06a2e0404b37f4954b86c7d168700faeaa18a1d3fb040e65f" :answer-field/choices ["q-138132/selection/05cdb65323b80c9fe96dd63e3181250fca61c519305420bd842eaeed4cc88a0f" "q-138132/selection/2916fcc150b54de80aa3d5d91f8869562405e4759e5b7f03de64a70da448efac" "q-138132/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-138132/selection/0dea6aa52ab2677bd869547653198fc6941279365f5067f18168268586cb5eaf" "q-138132/selection/e01ddc4ceb240ab06a2e0404b37f4954b86c7d168700faeaa18a1d3fb040e65f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-138132" :question/id #uuid "8ed6c9ae-9aab-55de-abb6-e10b4482e460" :question/math-academy-id "q-138132" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-138132/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-138132"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "049edd18-199a-5f23-9309-86d563f2250f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "fe085ac2-3ee4-5021-a88b-dab1453d6643" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer/id #uuid "33debe8b-9ce4-5f0e-a1a2-413a977f4dc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "844bdc6e-f8c2-5772-b505-1da6a0a6dd78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "c3be71cb-8cbd-5637-910d-7417920ab55f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9e2943-4dc9-5cf0-9c29-f0d8b3cd7f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170525/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-170525/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" "q-170525/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-170525/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" "q-170525/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-170525/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170525" :question/id #uuid "ed1100fb-29a5-505d-94d9-19a7ca9b0ff7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170525" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-12-\\lambda  & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-256 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -20)(\\lambda +20) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =20,\\,-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170525/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170525"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection/10480b80453a33526e5e2bb7565713551eb97e390066d414362a3cfb81183e0d" :answer/id #uuid "bfb6fa09-8f33-5cc1-924f-dccd9cbca049" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection/e932b457202dd50e3346686b4df5d9b9c6940598a706b103edba2bb441a2903b" :answer/id #uuid "2ba90dd4-3b1c-5597-b4d5-08ae59a07705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection/edec54291c545f8812f0d6e4895f5abe6ae3289765bac45e092bd4c8f8245681" :answer/id #uuid "7c2e5047-92fd-5f8b-8f3d-a331cb838801" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection/e815b839b018bdcf220649fd808c55addd89f934206f487865ac8e31f21c9e8c" :answer/id #uuid "12592a3b-58b0-5a9f-8760-bc61f1f63c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection/5104f04cfd4c67845a439c7e7679739daac54d38e8941c5c5c934bbc9c302b91" :answer/id #uuid "7f0eda24-a46d-5029-b0f2-14971b40116b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1f576a-1853-5717-9837-623e2d445033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-179407/selection/edec54291c545f8812f0d6e4895f5abe6ae3289765bac45e092bd4c8f8245681" :answer-field/choices ["q-179407/selection/10480b80453a33526e5e2bb7565713551eb97e390066d414362a3cfb81183e0d" "q-179407/selection/e932b457202dd50e3346686b4df5d9b9c6940598a706b103edba2bb441a2903b" "q-179407/selection/edec54291c545f8812f0d6e4895f5abe6ae3289765bac45e092bd4c8f8245681" "q-179407/selection/e815b839b018bdcf220649fd808c55addd89f934206f487865ac8e31f21c9e8c" "q-179407/selection/5104f04cfd4c67845a439c7e7679739daac54d38e8941c5c5c934bbc9c302b91"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-179407" :question/id #uuid "9517d41a-5a17-54a5-b8d2-6ea1cb7908a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-179407" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $12.$ Find a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n Notice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\n This is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\n the equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\n So, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\n we obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n \n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n As a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n Now, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $||y||=1,$ as usual.\n\n Since we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $12,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=12.\n$$\n\n Next, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=12:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-12I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]-12[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Seeking a non-zero solution of $(B-12I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\n Finally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-179407/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-179407"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "730d440c-5472-5ff1-8d63-44b1ba96d27a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "1af67308-26fb-5ebe-8a33-4baa7172a19a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection/d6944c4d51ee6e8916d5e2815a8478bad91a2f9e9e9bcd499b503b9fbcb165ab" :answer/id #uuid "26fdff92-959c-513e-b2d1-c5e6b133735a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "f073e011-7baf-56a1-b76e-c51a4d08f043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "8c9bd8aa-b20c-5125-b7dc-d7dc84792cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dffffdcc-8282-5863-b08a-0c8bc26c7cfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170526/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer-field/choices ["q-170526/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-170526/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" "q-170526/selection/d6944c4d51ee6e8916d5e2815a8478bad91a2f9e9e9bcd499b503b9fbcb165ab" "q-170526/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-170526/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170526" :question/id #uuid "586db336-c37b-5179-a537-c9794863823d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170526" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=8{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{4},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -8 \\\\ -16 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}32-\\lambda  & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (32-\\lambda )(-16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda -1\\,536 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +32) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=48.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection/0086db6077a62a5e2d9c771f7cdf57bea92b1de496b4d9b1d9c50dc1e5b58991" :answer/id #uuid "c85a8c04-5628-541e-8f77-f1ee13696883" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer/id #uuid "645080f5-2da5-518b-8c50-50a622ae6ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection/8642573b27c8383f67a645c39127977f20fbab89a940b170bac777f18f881b9b" :answer/id #uuid "266d4640-48a8-504c-8f2b-58ee34d3b014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection/a2ce0d7121680ad5ca64b109a32cd456c311ade4af21eb0a0dd652e8319117f1" :answer/id #uuid "3ed85064-5cda-584d-8015-984109e82263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection/f07223adf063c43bfac440bfcfa1f5e709d54202b4c2c93d5f069881800b6a64" :answer/id #uuid "ac4fb772-4fec-516f-990d-f42f935bc00f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e94b50cb-0b3a-5bbd-ae67-c67a107cfd12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170515/selection/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer-field/choices ["q-170515/selection/0086db6077a62a5e2d9c771f7cdf57bea92b1de496b4d9b1d9c50dc1e5b58991" "q-170515/selection/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" "q-170515/selection/8642573b27c8383f67a645c39127977f20fbab89a940b170bac777f18f881b9b" "q-170515/selection/a2ce0d7121680ad5ca64b109a32cd456c311ade4af21eb0a0dd652e8319117f1" "q-170515/selection/f07223adf063c43bfac440bfcfa1f5e709d54202b4c2c93d5f069881800b6a64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170515" :question/id #uuid "f11afa25-1308-5750-96f1-0ed1f6ce353b" :question/math-academy-id "q-170515" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170515/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170515"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186198611 :question/answer-fields 17592186194567] {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/16cad36e41fdba5121f5d643beb29ed713b7c2125b640151b54595c908f05731" :answer/id #uuid "dce92011-ba07-5ae2-92ed-dde47a1e2952" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/23ce5badce1b5381c3e058188f6d01b1a57540d1a68cc36db54b523b7f76d1f2" :answer/id #uuid "79809096-27a7-528f-a19f-3b201be19619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/fe736d169a35c41d19495d43ca0ff24f91a5dc9707f082e948947b9339f5e5e2" :answer/id #uuid "e1dbf8f9-5440-542e-9d6a-a687cc8a9404" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/d89a3ddb7cb64e9aea5cc220c979d6777857db50a2707e0e8ac7b5d076a466ef" :answer/id #uuid "f0ff9ae7-7119-5fd3-896a-4724bd01cb15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/ce8d6c8368d90cc9dd2c2fbabaea9248ada47bab5f08b664905440710eb1406f" :answer/id #uuid "c9d4ea07-ff1e-5ba6-9a51-9710f694813c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d41db1-23bb-58b3-ad08-0fedc717aae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/fe736d169a35c41d19495d43ca0ff24f91a5dc9707f082e948947b9339f5e5e2" :answer-field/choices ["q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/16cad36e41fdba5121f5d643beb29ed713b7c2125b640151b54595c908f05731" "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/23ce5badce1b5381c3e058188f6d01b1a57540d1a68cc36db54b523b7f76d1f2" "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/fe736d169a35c41d19495d43ca0ff24f91a5dc9707f082e948947b9339f5e5e2" "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/d89a3ddb7cb64e9aea5cc220c979d6777857db50a2707e0e8ac7b5d076a466ef" "q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0/ce8d6c8368d90cc9dd2c2fbabaea9248ada47bab5f08b664905440710eb1406f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-170138"] :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-170138/selection/ma-v1/6d8a89dc3721fcae9b2d25fa478ec6e60fb167d51ad412581984b399c3c877c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12663"] :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $0.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/613dd6029260150b75d9f037c947fbd3a3ab05cd63b74cb3ab29fdd4f2a0963c.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/dc259d743780ce58cf437ef11518a22a8eb551af9a934d2ad29bc418cdb98db7.png)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/a091f1b304062296158d63256cb19b2fe30b67340863b0f4355f9f0740d0bd71.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12662"] :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= -x_1^2+2x_2^2+6x_3^2+4x_1x_2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $6.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/3449b02c92b2b2534241d33f7b6288d8433eb907b5a97ad255aa9fd5022d5971.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/07bb866fbd14af52b5f3d01c91f6f58e1a7ef5bc4d70ada8c3a745035cae9e92.png)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/467bac248fa9988f4ee41bb9adaaf5e7ecbdf0e7e47b66a091ff5edf799e6799.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12664"] :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16,$ and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/fab48c5c9c4d9adee4f270c6138d0f69317cfa59ad9d6f5dab2d4cad15d368c6.png)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/3042cf8eadb699a70b70daced51411fa570ec9cdf3b7b270943fe790739a5364.png) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/df1ad3d9484608d37e762f74d2a61776c8f82f8eb650dcb0a37f47c5037678c4.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/c869aa1eb73c078eaac66201b1062b2d55a45d90ec2010b6af532ca601498a53.png) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
